 "verdicts": [
  "Headcount does not explain it: NITS has 274 research-active faculty, fewer than MANIT (296). Output per faculty does: 30.62 papers per person against 19.46 at MANIT and 15.5 at NITJ.",
  "It is broad-based, not a few prolific people: the median faculty member at NITS has 23 papers against 18 at MANIT and 13 at NITJ.",
  "Concentration: the top 10% of faculty hold 33.1% of paper credits at MANIT, 36.3% of paper credits at NITJ, 37.9% of paper credits at NITS (similar, so concentration is not what separates the three institutes).",
  "Shared papers are not inflating it: splitting each paper between its faculty authors leaves NITS at 30.56 papers per faculty against 19.43 at MANIT and 15.41 at NITJ.",
  "Team size (3.4-3.6 authors per paper) and conference share (33.2-37.6%) are similar across the three institutes, so these measures do not explain the gap.",
  "Seniority: NITS has 10.6% professors against 26.7% at MANIT and 9.1% at NITJ, yet its professors average 86.7 papers each (MANIT 29.5, NITJ 37.2).",
  "Like for like: NITS has the highest papers per faculty in 6 of the 8 departments that exist at all three institutes.",
  "NITS published the most papers in 10 of the 10 full years (2016-2025)."
 ],
